1. What is the difference between a feature and a label?
A feature is an input variable used by the model. A label is the known answer that a supervised model learns to predict.
Q2. Why do we split data into training and testing sets?
To evaluate whether a model generalizes to unseen examples instead of measuring only how well it performs on data it has already learned from.
Q3. What is random_state?
It controls the randomness of a split or algorithm so results can be reproduced when the same data and settings are used.
Q4. What is stratify=y?
It helps preserve the target class proportions across the splits. It is useful for classification, especially when one class is less common than another, provided the class counts permit the split.
Q5. Can we use the test dataset to tune the model repeatedly?
We should avoid this. Repeatedly tuning decisions based on test results can indirectly overfit the test set. Use validation data or cross-validation for model selection, then reserve the test set for final evaluation.
Q6. What is the difference between overfitting and data leakage?
- Overfitting: The model learns the training data too closely, including noise, and performs poorly on new data.
- Data leakage: Unavailable or inappropriate information influences model training or evaluation, making results misleading.
They are different problems, though they can sometimes occur together.
